1
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:12,560
‘How do you get to school?’

2
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:22,060
This question often gets an answer like ‘By bus’ or ‘I walk’ or ‘My parents take me by car’.

3
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:34,180
But not always – there are children in many different parts of the world who, every day, have to go on a difficult journey in order to get to their lessons.

4
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:42,000
They travel for kilometres on foot, or by boat, bicycle, donkey or train.

5
00:00:42,640 --> 00:01:01,440
They cross deserts, mountains, rivers, snow and ice: for example, the children of the Iñupiat community in Alaska go to school and then come back when it is dark, in extremely cold temperatures.

6
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,925
And they are not the only ones.

7
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:08,180
Kids in many countries do this and more.

8
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:19,203
These children in Indonesia have to cross a bridge ten metres above a dangerous river to get to their class on time.

9
00:01:19,203 --> 00:01:24,620
(Some years ago the bridge fell down after very heavy rain.)

10
00:01:25,519 --> 00:01:31,920
Then they walk many more kilometres through the forest to their school in Banten.

11
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:43,143
A pupil at Gulu Village Primary School, China, rides a donkey as his grandfather walks beside him.

12
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,540
Gulu is a mountain village in a national park.

13
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,224
The school is far away from the village.

14
00:01:51,600 --> 00:02:00,628
It is halfway up a mountain, so it takes five hours to climb from the bottom of the mountain to the school.

15
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:09,138
The children have a dangerous journey: the path is only 45 centimetres wide in some places.

16
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:18,900
In Sri Lanka, some children have to cross a piece of wood between two walls of an old castle every morning.

17
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:22,060
Their teacher watches them carefully.

18
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:32,067
But in Galle, Sri Lanka, many girls don’t go to school – they have to go to work or get married young.

19
00:02:32,067 --> 00:02:37,500
So girls are happy to take a risk in order to get to school.

20
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:55,320
Six-year-old Fabricio Oliveira gets on his donkey every morning to ride with his friends for over an hour through a desert region in the very dry Sertão area of northeast Brazil.

21
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:58,640
Their school is in Extrema.

22
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:02,880
It’s a tiny village – very few people live there.

23
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:09,240
These children live in houses on Chetla Road in Delhi, India.

24
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:16,200
Their homes are near the busy and dangerous railway lines that go to Alipur station.

25
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,940
Every morning they walk along the tracks to get to their school, 40 minutes away.

26
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:29,792
So one question we can ask is: why do the children do this?

27
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,962
Because their parents make them do it?

28
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:49,309
The answer, in many cases, is no – it’s because for them going to school means a better future: they hope to get a job and money so they can help their families and their neighbours.

29
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:56,960
And this is why rivers, deserts or danger won’t stop them on their way to school.
